WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(upbeat music)

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.680
- All right.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.913
Welcome, everyone.

00:00:17.913 --> 00:00:19.860
As I said and mentioned a moment ago,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:20.693
my name's Alfred Guy

00:00:20.693 --> 00:00:21.989
and I work in the Poorvu Center

00:00:21.989 --> 00:00:24.600
as the Director of Undergraduate Tutoring.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.030
I also oversee the writing
requirement in Yale College,

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.400
and I teach courses in
English and humanities.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.233
I'm gonna give about

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:31.500
a two minute introduction to this panel,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.470
and then we're gonna get
right to the faculty speakers.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.500
Within a week of
ChatGPT-3.5 being released

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:38.333
on November 30th,

00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:41.220
the Poorvu Center started to
get questions from faculty

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.128
about its implications for teaching,

00:00:43.128 --> 00:00:45.962
because this technology is
potentially so disruptive,

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:48.205
which I don't mean as pejorative,

00:00:48.205 --> 00:00:49.410
for teaching and learning,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.520
we all have questions,

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.980
and so it made sense to
gather people to talk.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.990
We're very grateful to the faculty panel

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.770
for agreeing to launch this conversation,

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.334
which we expect will also
continue well after this event.

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:02.310
The format for the event is

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.130
that each faculty panelist
will speak for five minutes

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.124
about their ideas

00:01:06.124 --> 00:01:08.700
for how AI generative writing

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.450
might affect teaching and learning.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.600
After the panelists,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.370
we will have time to hear some questions

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.840
that were submitted in advance

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.673
and some questions

00:01:16.673 --> 00:01:20.727
that people generate during
this meeting via chat.

00:01:20.727 --> 00:01:23.730
We may get more questions
than we can use today,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.920
but we will use them to
shape future programming

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.241
and resources that we
build about these issues.

00:01:28.241 --> 00:01:30.990
I wanna welcome Nisheeth Vishnoi

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.850
from the Computer Science Department.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:33.870
Professor Vishnoi is

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.110
the A. Bartlett Giamatti
Professor of Computer Science

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.017
and a co-founder

00:01:38.017 --> 00:01:40.679
of the Computation and Society Initiative

00:01:40.679 --> 00:01:42.750
at Yale University.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.760
He studies the foundations of computation

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.310
and his research spends several areas

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.902
of theoretical computer science,

00:01:48.902 --> 00:01:51.363
optimization, and machine learning.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.473
He's also interested in
understanding nature and society

00:01:55.473 --> 00:01:58.140
from a computational viewpoint.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.390
Here his current focus includes studying

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.430
the emergency of intelligent...

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.340
Excuse me, that's a Freudian slip.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.726
Studying the emergence
of intelligent behavior

00:02:07.726 --> 00:02:09.270
and ethical problems

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.394
at the interface of artificial
intelligence and society.

00:02:12.394 --> 00:02:14.313
Professor Vishnoi, welcome.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.190
- Thank you, Alfie.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.594
Yes, I guess it was a Freudian slip.

00:02:19.594 --> 00:02:23.130
We are in some kind of
an emergency, I guess,

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:23.963
and I'm very glad

00:02:23.963 --> 00:02:27.000
to see so many people
interested in this event.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.110
So I'll keep my remarks
brief and to the point.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:35.430
I guess we are all here
to talk about ChatGPT,

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.890
which is one of the latest in the line

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:41.334
of what are called
generative models in AI.

00:02:41.334 --> 00:02:46.334
So these are algorithms
which can learn about things

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.800
such as images, or in this case, texts.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.530
and are able to do
conversations with human beings,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.890
and I guess what has caught
our attention here is

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.190
the ability to have these kinds

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:05.010
of very non-trivial
interactions with human beings,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.024
and that's caused a lot of concern.

00:03:07.024 --> 00:03:08.700
So from my point of view,

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.770
I think coming to the point

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.863
of how it impacts the
teaching and learning,

00:03:14.832 --> 00:03:16.920
I think the first point
I would like to say is

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.920
that it'll add to showing us the gap

00:03:22.847 --> 00:03:25.773
between learning and evaluation.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.028
I guess, as a university,

00:03:29.028 --> 00:03:33.261
our goal is to ensure
that our students learn,

00:03:33.261 --> 00:03:34.590
but how we check

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.200
whether they have learned
these three evaluations,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.470
and I guess the concern that arises is

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.590
how students are going to use this tool

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.090
or tools such as this,

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.610
which are likely to come in future

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.940
to aid in their evaluation.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.670
So my prognosis is that in the short-term,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.343
I would imagine that
this gap will increase,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.650
but I think it does give
us a very nice opportunity

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.470
to think about how we can
reduce the misalignment

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.273
between learning and evaluation.

00:04:07.306 --> 00:04:08.893
The second point I would make is

00:04:08.893 --> 00:04:12.813
about how it can impact the power

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.560
and biases already present in society.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.720
We don't have to take
the example of ChatGPT,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.910
but even previous tools

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.130
from natural language
processing and computer vision

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:26.963
have illustrated

00:04:26.963 --> 00:04:31.963
that these algorithms can
propagate certain types of biases,

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.260
enabling or increasing the power

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:35.883
of certain groups in society,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.406
and I believe these kinds
of tools like ChatGPT

00:04:40.406 --> 00:04:41.880
will work both ways.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:42.713
On the one hand,

00:04:42.713 --> 00:04:44.460
they will level the playing field.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.363
For example,

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:49.710
let's say, if English is
not your natural language

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.140
and you would like to compose a nice email

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.480
to your professor or to whoever,

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.250
then you can put in a
partially phrased email,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:02.196
or not necessarily the finished email,

00:05:02.196 --> 00:05:04.470
and get a polished version of it,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.540
and you can imagine these kinds of users,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.960
which will reduce the gap
between different groups

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:10.950
who, for whatever reason,

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.442
have to spend more resources coming up

00:05:12.442 --> 00:05:14.253
with the same outcome.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:16.523
On the other hand,

00:05:16.523 --> 00:05:17.836
given that these are generative models,

00:05:17.836 --> 00:05:20.550
which are learning from existing data,

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.230
which data doesn't come from vacuum,

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.510
it comes from the society that we live in,

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:29.510
it will for sure propagate
the bias that already exists,

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.200
and I think things have to be done

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.053
to check these kinds of biases.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.820
I guess, one of the things

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:47.300
that might be also of immediate concern is

00:05:47.424 --> 00:05:49.800
that if students start using ChatGPT,

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.658
how are we going to check and enforce

00:05:53.658 --> 00:05:56.209
whether there is some kind of cheating

00:05:56.209 --> 00:05:57.630
in some way or the other?

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:01.200
This probably applies to exams

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.840
which have a more written component to it,

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.840
and I believe that that's
something that ChatGPT itself is,

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:13.018
or open AI itself is thinking about,

00:06:13.018 --> 00:06:15.330
how to include watermarking,

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.430
which is a technique
from computer science,

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:21.290
which allows you to spot
if certain answers are...

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:29.750
Have been generated using this
software or this algorithm.

00:06:30.349 --> 00:06:32.279
Yeah, so I think these are the key points

00:06:32.279 --> 00:06:34.410
and I will pass it on to the others,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:35.460
and I'm curious to hear

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.700
what my other colleagues and
audience would have to say.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:39.533
Thank you.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:41.940
- That's wonderful.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.983
Thank you so much, Professor Vishnoi.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.240
So our second panelist is
gonna be Brian Scassellati,

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.343
also from the Computer Science Department.

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:53.760
Professor Scassellati is the
A. Bartlett Giamatti professor

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.860
of computer science, cognitive science,

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.635
and mechanical engineering.

00:06:57.635 --> 00:07:00.210
He builds robots that help people

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.580
by providing social and cognitive support,

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:04.980
including systems that teach social skills

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.630
to children with autism,

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.870
systems that help adults cope
with anxiety and depression,

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:12.660
and systems that tutor children
in challenging subjects.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.750
His work not only produces useful tools,

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.110
but also helps us to learn about the ways

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.910
in which people understand
social behavior,

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.460
categorize things as
animate and inanimate,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.203
and collaborate with each other.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.390
So thank you and welcome
Professor Scassellati.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.870
- Thanks very much, Alfie,

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.640
and thanks, Nisheeth,

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.973
for giving us a really good
start and foundation for this.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.073
I guess my first message

00:07:38.073 --> 00:07:42.330
was really inspired by a lot
of the teachers and educators

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:43.530
that I spoke to recently,

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.037
and it's simply this,

00:07:45.037 --> 00:07:48.663
don't panic, we've been here before.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.680
In 1966, Joe Weizenbaum wrote
this program called ELIZA,

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.066
that, at the time,

00:07:56.066 --> 00:07:59.970
was thought of as this
incredible new thing,

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.590
but even Weizenbaum admitted

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.060
it was a very, very simple program.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:06.356
All it did was take in
a line of typed text

00:08:06.356 --> 00:08:08.588
and look for certain patterns,

00:08:08.588 --> 00:08:11.550
and replace one set of words with another.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.109
So if you said to ELIZA,

00:08:14.109 --> 00:08:16.680
"I'm really worried about my father,"

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.020
it would pick out the word
"worried" and "my father,"

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:19.860
and it would write back,

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:23.247
and say, "Why are you
worried about your father?"

00:08:24.716 --> 00:08:27.660
And it followed this school of thought

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:31.620
that's now identified as
Rogerian psychotherapy,

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:33.930
where the idea was to mirror back

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.363
without adding anything
to the conversation,

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:38.629
and at the time,

00:08:38.629 --> 00:08:40.440
many people thought of this

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.610
as almost a solution to natural language.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:45.510
People were worried

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.100
that this was going to
replace them in their jobs.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:52.260
The Association for
Psychotherapy wrote Weizenbaum

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.840
and begged him to get rid of the program.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.750
Weizenbaum's own assistant he had to fire,

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:01.328
because she did nothing other
than talk to ELIZA all day.

00:09:01.328 --> 00:09:04.020
Even serious natural language researchers,

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.747
this brand new field that had just begun,

00:09:06.747 --> 00:09:08.310
some of them wrote

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.890
that probably they were
only a few years away

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:12.630
from solving language,

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:13.653
whatever that meant.

00:09:14.718 --> 00:09:17.010
We've seen this before

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:18.510
and there's always a lot of hype,

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:22.530
and excitement that comes when
new technologies come out.

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:23.490
Now that's not to say

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:27.540
that ChatGPT isn't an
amazing piece of technology,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:31.254
but we've faced these problems before.

00:09:31.254 --> 00:09:36.254
In 2015, I taught the first
version of CS50 here at Yale

00:09:36.291 --> 00:09:41.070
and we had almost 425
students in the class.

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:41.903
I think it is

00:09:41.903 --> 00:09:44.580
still the largest engineering
course we've ever taught here,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:46.433
and when we did,

00:09:46.433 --> 00:09:49.290
we realized that between 400 students here

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.210
and 800 students at Harvard,

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:53.710
we were gonna have problems
with academic honesty,

00:09:54.655 --> 00:09:57.477
and along the way we developed some tools,

00:09:57.477 --> 00:10:00.750
and we used some existing
tools out there to help us,

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:02.850
because there were already at the time,

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.250
huge databases of code,

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:09.206
and even completed assignments
that were available online,

00:10:09.206 --> 00:10:11.970
not to mention forums like Stack Exchange,

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:12.920
where you could post a question

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:15.750
and someone would helpfully
give you a piece of code

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.070
as an answer,

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:20.100
or even automated systems like Copilot

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:23.127
that would write code along with you.

00:10:23.127 --> 00:10:27.384
So how could we distinguish
these automatically generated

00:10:27.384 --> 00:10:32.384
or just plain plagiarized
pieces from everything else?

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:33.900
Now, for us, at the time,

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:37.119
one of the key things was we
looked at the white space,

00:10:37.119 --> 00:10:39.570
that is the difference

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:42.192
between spaces, and tabs, and line fields

00:10:42.192 --> 00:10:45.153
in the code that was written,

00:10:45.153 --> 00:10:48.060
and in fact, it was very
easy to find the students

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:49.940
who had copied things directly,

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.590
because those patterns were very obvious

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:54.808
and very easy to spot.

00:10:54.808 --> 00:10:57.516
That's not gonna be the same with ChatGPT.

00:10:57.516 --> 00:11:01.020
It's not gonna be as easy
for us to go in and look,

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.720
and say, "Are there the
right number of spaces

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:07.192
in between that period and
the start of the next line?"

00:11:07.192 --> 00:11:08.913
But there are patterns.

00:11:10.208 --> 00:11:11.372
I think overall,

00:11:11.372 --> 00:11:15.372
the second message I would wanna
bring today is just to say,

00:11:15.372 --> 00:11:17.220
in a way, this is a challenge.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.670
It is a challenge for us as educators,

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.490
but I think it's actually
a very good challenge,

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:24.630
because it's forcing us

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.934
to look for creative and original thought,

00:11:27.934 --> 00:11:31.380
and not just what these systems do,

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.300
which is just a rewording

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:37.383
and a regurgitation of the
sources that they have been fed.

00:11:39.105 --> 00:11:41.250
I think that in the whole,

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:46.250
this will make us a stronger
and better organization,

00:11:46.265 --> 00:11:49.360
a way in which we can train our students

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:52.800
to be more robust thinkers,

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.993
but it will be more effort along the way.

00:11:56.264 --> 00:12:00.240
So there is a challenge
out there for us right now.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:01.192
How do we look at the way

00:12:01.192 --> 00:12:04.800
that we take these written submissions

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:05.760
and evaluate them,

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.413
not just based on how they
can replay sources from us,

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:14.613
but rather look for that
creative and original thought.

00:12:15.981 --> 00:12:17.313
Thanks very much.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:20.093
- Thank you so much.

00:12:20.093 --> 00:12:21.150
That was wonderful.

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:21.983
Thank you.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:25.980
All right, so I'm gonna
introduce Laura Wexler,

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.290
our next speaker.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:29.400
Professor Wexler is

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.339
the Charles H. Farnam
Professor of American Studies

00:12:32.339 --> 00:12:35.580
and Women's, Gender,
and Sexuality Studies,

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.880
the acting co-chair of the
Yale Public Humanities Program,

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:41.250
and a founding member of FemTechNet,

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:43.413
the Feminist Technology Network.

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:46.680
A scholar of photography
and visual culture,

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:49.751
she is the founding PI,
principal investigator,

00:12:49.751 --> 00:12:52.200
of Photogrammar.

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:55.770
an interactive, multi-layer geospatial map

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:58.470
of the more than 170,000 photographs

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:00.600
and metadata taken and collected

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.730
by the Farm Security Administration

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:04.950
between 1935 and 1944

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.350
and housed at the Library of Congress.

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:09.450
She's interested in all aspects

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:10.682
of the digital learning space.

00:13:10.682 --> 00:13:13.230
So thank you and welcome,
Professor Wexler.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:14.328
- Oh, thank you very much.

00:13:14.328 --> 00:13:18.480
I'm so pleased that this is happening

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.158
and thank you, Alfie,

00:13:20.158 --> 00:13:22.500
and Jenny Frederick, and the Poorvu Center

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:25.200
for allowing us to have this opportunity

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.200
for opening this discussion
together as a community,

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:29.590
and I thank my two colleagues

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:32.550
whose remarks were very much, I think,

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.383
on point and really helpful.

00:13:35.666 --> 00:13:39.870
It does seem to me also
that we've been here before,

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:42.162
cell phones and computers, Wikipedia,

00:13:42.162 --> 00:13:44.700
Google search, spell check.

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.280
Just in the last few years in the academy,

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:48.550
there have been a lot of changes

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:50.070
that have been said

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:53.059
to herald the end of
things as we know them,

00:13:53.059 --> 00:13:56.730
and that we had to put
up defensive barriers

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:58.604
in our classrooms against them,

00:13:58.604 --> 00:14:02.250
as somehow I have a contrarian feeling

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:07.200
that leads me all the way
back to a very ancient story

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:09.963
that I wanna share with you today.

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:13.320
So this is just pieces of this story.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:14.929
A long time ago,

00:14:14.929 --> 00:14:19.279
a famous teacher told this
story to one of his pupils.

00:14:19.279 --> 00:14:22.710
He was talking about an Egyptian god

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:24.315
who had invented writing

00:14:24.315 --> 00:14:26.910
and was trying to tell the king of thieves

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:30.150
about how he invented writing,

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:31.327
and he said,

00:14:31.327 --> 00:14:33.450
"It will make the Egyptians wiser

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:35.266
and will improve their memory.

00:14:35.266 --> 00:14:39.810
I have discovered a potion
for memory and for wisdom,"

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:40.897
and the king replied,

00:14:40.897 --> 00:14:42.797
"Oh, most expert, Theuth,

00:14:42.797 --> 00:14:47.089
one man can give birth to
the elements of an art,

00:14:47.089 --> 00:14:48.990
but only another can judge

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:51.703
how they can benefit or harm
those who will use them,"

00:14:51.703 --> 00:14:55.171
and now, since you are
the father of writing,

00:14:55.171 --> 00:14:56.820
your affection for it

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:58.831
has made you describe its effects

00:14:58.831 --> 00:15:01.980
as the opposite of what they really are.

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:05.340
In fact, writing will
introduce forgetfulness

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.010
into the soul of those who learn it.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.710
They will not practice using their memory,

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:13.230
because they will put
their trust in writing,

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:14.352
which is external

00:15:14.352 --> 00:15:17.597
and depends on signs
that belong to others.

00:15:17.597 --> 00:15:19.830
Instead of trying to remember

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:22.757
from the inside, completely on their own.

00:15:22.757 --> 00:15:26.010
You have not discovered
a potion for remembering,

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:27.959
but for reminding.

00:15:27.959 --> 00:15:31.680
You provide your students
with the appearance of wisdom,

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:33.930
not with its reality.

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:35.640
Your invention will enable them

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:39.150
to hear many things without
being properly taught,

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:41.780
and they will imagine that
they have come to know much,

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.072
while, for the most part,

00:15:43.072 --> 00:15:44.530
they will know nothing

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:47.520
and they will be difficult
to get along with,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:50.028
since they will merely appear to be wise

00:15:50.028 --> 00:15:52.890
instead of really being so."

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:54.930
This is, of course, Socrates,

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:56.052
and this is the "Phaedrus,"

00:15:56.052 --> 00:15:58.500
the dialogue that Plato wrote down,

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.157
because Socrates wouldn't use writing

00:16:00.157 --> 00:16:02.666
to write his own teachings down.

00:16:02.666 --> 00:16:06.150
Your students will not
learn from the inside

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:08.550
and they will be hard to get along with,

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:11.130
because they will imagine
that they know something

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.173
when actually they know nothing,

00:16:13.173 --> 00:16:15.060
and what this is,

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:16.204
I think at base,

00:16:16.204 --> 00:16:19.140
underneath the sensationalism
of all the headlines,

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:20.940
is the college essay over?

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:22.590
Is the college class over?

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.210
Is the college education over?

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:25.920
Are college instructors over?

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:26.753
Is this over?

00:16:26.753 --> 00:16:28.428
Which sells papers.

00:16:28.428 --> 00:16:31.320
That's why we're being
asked these questions.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:34.598
At base, this is very much the same fear.

00:16:34.598 --> 00:16:39.598
If you commit learning to writing,

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.697
students will not know from the inside,

00:16:42.697 --> 00:16:45.090
but what I want to focus on

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:49.080
about this very interesting,
ancient dialogue is

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.764
that Socrates' solution

00:16:51.764 --> 00:16:55.290
does not lie in a clever assignment,

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:56.790
or a brilliant prompt,

00:16:56.790 --> 00:17:00.690
or a way of keeping the collaborative AI

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:03.960
out of the space of the classroom.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:08.820
It actually relies on
person to person presence

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:11.404
between people learning together,

00:17:11.404 --> 00:17:13.355
teachers and students,

00:17:13.355 --> 00:17:16.440
so that the writing is

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:20.439
merely a reminder of the embodied, real,

00:17:20.439 --> 00:17:23.640
I, thou, another
philosopher would've said,

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:27.720
knowledge that's being
communicated in the classroom,

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:30.240
and to me, I would like to focus

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:33.210
on this aspect of the challenge

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.580
and the opportunity that is before us,

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:40.580
because it's very exciting
what the AI can now do,

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:43.920
and very exciting to learn.

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:45.870
It's not that we're being confronted

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:49.500
with the strange, alien
presence of a computer

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:51.630
that will now take over our classrooms.

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:53.100
We're actually being confronted

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:55.832
with a new aspect of ourselves,

00:17:55.832 --> 00:17:57.781
patterns at scale

00:17:57.781 --> 00:17:59.460
that we've never been able

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.550
to understand about ourselves before.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:04.440
This is the challenge.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:09.440
It will certainly change what
we know and what we can learn,

00:18:09.898 --> 00:18:12.660
but I don't believe it will change

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:15.960
the very basic key to knowledge

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:19.020
that Socrates was trying to explain,

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:21.219
person to person excitement

00:18:21.219 --> 00:18:25.710
about transmitting learning together.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:29.490
So I see this as an immense opportunity

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:33.694
to discover with our students together

00:18:33.694 --> 00:18:37.140
what we can do with this new technology.

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:37.973
Thank you.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:42.329
- Thank you so much, Professor Wexler.

00:18:42.329 --> 00:18:43.713
Thank you very much.

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:47.788
So rich already.

00:18:47.788 --> 00:18:50.889
I have so many thoughts and feelings.

00:18:50.889 --> 00:18:54.750
Our next panelist is Justin
Farrell from sociology.

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:57.000
So Justin Farrell is a
professor of sociology

00:18:57.000 --> 00:18:58.797
in the Yale School of the Environment.

00:18:58.797 --> 00:19:00.686
He studies American culture

00:19:00.686 --> 00:19:04.140
with a focus on social
class, moral conflict,

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:06.600
epistemology, and the environment.

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:09.316
He blends ethnographic
field work in rural areas

00:19:09.316 --> 00:19:11.940
with large scale computational techniques

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:14.970
from network science and machine learning.

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:17.160
So welcome and thank
you, Professor Farrell.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:18.330
- Thank you so much.

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.160
It's great to be here.

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.200
Before I talk specifically about

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:24.960
the ways that I'm responding
to AI in my teaching,

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:28.080
I wanna say a few words
about knowledge production,

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:31.623
about ethics, and the
mission of higher education,

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:34.800
to echo Dr. Wexler's comments as well.

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:38.733
So allow me to be very
big picture for a moment.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.330
I know it might sound trite,

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:43.470
I guess, maybe not,

00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:48.180
but before we start to devise
fixes or technical solutions,

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.590
I think we need to think harder

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:52.290
about the kind of culture that exists

00:19:52.290 --> 00:19:53.851
in the modern university

00:19:53.851 --> 00:19:57.033
and how that affects the role of AI today.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:02.310
All fields from the medical
and planetary sciences

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:04.435
to the social sciences and humanities,

00:20:04.435 --> 00:20:08.250
they rely on agreed upon
rules and practices,

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:12.660
educational virtues that are
necessary for truth seeking,

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:16.020
and they favor disagreement, argument,

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:19.620
conflict, continual
openness to new evidence,

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:21.953
and I would ask, "Are we
cultivating these virtues

00:20:21.953 --> 00:20:23.460
and how can we do better?"

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:26.428
And I think this needs to
be part of the conversation.

00:20:26.428 --> 00:20:30.236
For example, in what ways
are teaching and learning,

00:20:30.236 --> 00:20:32.587
even in the most technical fields,

00:20:32.587 --> 00:20:35.850
captive to instrumental thinking?

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:36.758
Are we limiting our students

00:20:36.758 --> 00:20:41.100
with surface level or
instrumentalized visions

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:42.895
of learning and knowledge production.

00:20:42.895 --> 00:20:45.070
These issues are really big

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:48.900
and they're ancient as
Dr. Wexler just showed,

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:52.650
and I'm not naive to the
social and economic pressures,

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:55.787
but all fields from econ to medicine,

00:20:55.787 --> 00:20:59.010
to business, sociology, computer science

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:00.778
can, in their own way,

00:21:00.778 --> 00:21:03.690
instill values of a liberal education

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:06.160
that I think will combat a culture

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:11.850
whereby cheating feels
necessary or even justified,

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:13.681
because only education

00:21:13.681 --> 00:21:16.650
can nurture a culture of truth seeking,

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:18.260
of scientific reasoning,

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:22.740
and ethical engagement needed
to inoculate individuals

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:26.370
from falling prey to the misuse of AI.

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:28.200
So as AI continues to improve,

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:30.810
I think professors and teachers need

00:21:30.810 --> 00:21:35.310
to bring a renewed seriousness
to education itself,

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:37.470
and Poorvu is a big help in this,

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:42.030
so that we might build wisdom
and character in our students

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:44.460
as they write papers,
as they do experiments,

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:47.130
visit the archives, write computer code,

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:49.410
and just become producers of knowledge.

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:52.800
So I hope all this can be part
of the conversation as well.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:57.480
So more concretely, how is
AI influencing my teaching?

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.703
I just have two points on this.

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:04.645
One way I think it's
becoming more common is

00:22:04.645 --> 00:22:07.263
subjecting these AI tools to critique.

00:22:08.215 --> 00:22:10.951
So I teach a field course this semester

00:22:10.951 --> 00:22:13.877
and when ChatGPT came online,

00:22:13.877 --> 00:22:17.207
I think it was in late
November, early December,

00:22:17.207 --> 00:22:19.567
I added to my syllabus

00:22:19.567 --> 00:22:23.700
that students must investigate
their research question

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:24.810
using ChatGPT,

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:27.300
but also critique it,

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:28.620
and learning through critique,

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:31.167
as we have always done in the academy.

00:22:31.167 --> 00:22:34.050
I identify kind of two
pathways for critique,

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:36.179
but there there are more.

00:22:36.179 --> 00:22:37.530
One would obviously be

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:40.860
to critique the accuracy
of ChatGPT essays,

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:42.564
relative to their research question.

00:22:42.564 --> 00:22:44.053
A lot of my students have

00:22:44.053 --> 00:22:47.490
on the ground research
questions for this class.

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:50.430
It's very applied in a lot of ways.

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:52.860
So, are the answers
that it gives accurate?

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:55.473
Are they misleading, unethical?

00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:58.110
What is missing?

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:01.080
What does it get right or partially right?

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:05.550
The second critique would be
the eventual commodification

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:08.250
or current commodification of the tool.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:10.680
So I see this as an opportunity

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:14.460
or an empirical question for
research for my students.

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:15.600
As we all know,

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.210
commercial interests monetize AI

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:20.250
and cash in on these algorithms

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:22.470
that can be destructive to social life.

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.660
So I'm pushing students to consider

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:28.200
how the production of knowledge
from tools like ChatGPT

00:23:28.200 --> 00:23:31.290
might parallel similar developments

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:34.350
where algorithms can
profit from disinformation,

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:37.770
from hyperbole or extremism,

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:39.000
or historically,

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:43.110
how is this similar to or
different from previous examples,

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:47.520
or even pre-digital campaigns
like smoking or climate denial

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:50.280
where powerful commercial
interests influence the production

00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:52.650
and dissemination of knowledge

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:54.150
and information to the public.

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:57.930
Secondly, other than critiquing,

00:23:57.930 --> 00:23:59.880
one way that that I think is,

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:02.250
perhaps more effective might be

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:04.680
prioritizing experiential learning,

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:07.650
and keeping with this course
I'm teaching this semester,

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:09.420
I try to get students out of the classroom

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:10.710
as much as possible

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:14.130
into the field using
project-based teaching

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:16.860
that I think offer tools for discovery,

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:19.860
tools for cognitive engagement, obviously,

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:21.780
but also social belonging,

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:24.660
and often with people
outside the university

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:26.460
who are very different than them,

00:24:26.460 --> 00:24:29.190
and so part of this
course over spring break,

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:32.280
we go to an area where I do research,

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:34.620
which is the greater
Yellowstone ecosystem,

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:37.590
and I bring my students
to Wyoming and Montana.

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:40.110
Now they have these research projects,

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:44.190
but they're paired with
local NGOs oftentimes,

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:47.040
and so they develop a relationship

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:50.460
and while they're using
ChatGPT for their projects,

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:53.589
they cultivate these virtues
of social responsibility,

00:24:53.589 --> 00:24:56.007
of personal investment in their projects

00:24:56.007 --> 00:24:58.860
and responsibility to their
on the ground partners,

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:02.010
and so I think, at the present moment,

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:03.300
this type of encounter

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:05.340
and these type of experiences

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:08.253
are difficult to replicate even for AI.

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:10.457
So I'll leave it there.

00:25:10.457 --> 00:25:11.313
Thank you.

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:13.590
- That's great.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:16.050
Thank you so much, Professor Farrell,

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:18.240
and so we'll hear from one more panelist

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:20.190
and then we have some questions
that have been coming in.

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:22.140
Amazing questions by the way, people.

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:23.390
You keep sending them in.

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:27.480
Okay, so we're gonna hear
from Professor Khawaja,

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:30.120
Noreen Khawaja from the
Religious Studies Department.

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:31.001
Professor Khawaja teaches

00:25:31.001 --> 00:25:34.560
in the religion and modernity
area of religious studies,

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:36.810
working at the intersection of philosophy,

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:40.740
religious thought, and culture
in the modern Euro-Americas.

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:44.040
Her work deals with social
practices of authenticity

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.320
and with the effects and concepts

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:48.600
that structure our experience of the real.

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:50.411
In fact, I think this is FX

00:25:50.411 --> 00:25:53.550
and concepts that structure
our experience of the real.

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:57.150
Okay, thank you so much,
Professor Khawaja, and welcome.

00:25:57.150 --> 00:26:01.320
- Thank you so much for the
inviting me to be on this panel.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:02.880
I've already learned a great deal

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:04.230
from all those who've spoken

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:07.140
and it is a very exciting opportunity

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:08.610
to talk about these things.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:10.680
I think, in the interest

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:13.411
of not repeating some
of those in the panel

00:26:13.411 --> 00:26:14.325
who've already gone

00:26:14.325 --> 00:26:17.100
and who've said things that I agree with,

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:20.010
I'll say that referring back
to a few of those comments,

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:20.843
I think...

00:26:20.843 --> 00:26:22.233
Well, I'll start by saying,

00:26:23.413 --> 00:26:27.240
I speak not as an in any way an expert

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:29.280
on artificial intelligence, or technology,

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:31.200
or the history of technology in any way,

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:32.760
and I speak also as a teacher

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:34.290
at a particular kind of university

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:36.000
that we are all inhabiting.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:38.430
It's not the same how we might
approach these questions,

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:40.620
I think for Yale undergraduate
students, for example,

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:43.233
than if we were teaching
at a larger public school

00:26:43.233 --> 00:26:46.230
with a different kind
of student population,

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:48.240
and perhaps, because of that,

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:50.040
perhaps, because of the
kinds of things I teach

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:51.420
in the humanities,

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:52.650
the questions about

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:54.960
checking for cheating
and that sort of thing,

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:57.330
I think play a little
bit of a different role

00:26:57.330 --> 00:27:01.140
in the way that I think
about these questions.

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:02.100
It doesn't track, I think,

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:04.403
alongside some of those
who've spoken already,

00:27:04.403 --> 00:27:05.910
it doesn't track with some of the way

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:07.710
that the headlines are framing the issues,

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:11.520
especially, I think,
difficult to talk about

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:13.380
are two aspects.

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:17.644
One is to do, I think, with
the broader era that we're in.

00:27:17.644 --> 00:27:20.880
We've been here before is
one of the themes I think

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:22.650
that people have been mentioning,

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:26.160
and that's so both in the sense
of a particular panic about

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:27.810
a particular kind of tool,

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:30.330
which will introduce
particular possibilities

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:33.150
which we can't totally map at present

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:34.170
and the kind of anxiety

00:27:34.170 --> 00:27:36.480
that builds around that particular event,

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:38.310
but then a kind of broader anxiety,

00:27:38.310 --> 00:27:39.270
which I think we're seeing

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:42.296
in so many different registers
of social life today,

00:27:42.296 --> 00:27:45.511
which is kind of an ontological confusion.

00:27:45.511 --> 00:27:49.025
What's real, what's mine, what's yours?

00:27:49.025 --> 00:27:53.417
How the kinds of knowledges
that we practice are made.

00:27:53.417 --> 00:27:54.682
There's a lot of...

00:27:54.682 --> 00:27:57.724
I think the conversations
that we're having about AI

00:27:57.724 --> 00:28:01.380
are maybe particular condensations

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:02.460
of a lot of the conversations

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:04.708
that we've been having
about facts and fictions,

00:28:04.708 --> 00:28:06.640
and the very fact that

00:28:08.250 --> 00:28:09.750
all these different kinds of tools

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:11.670
that we might describe as AI,

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:14.430
people who've worked on the
meaning of that category,

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:17.520
but just so far as we are
talking about it for ourselves

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:18.930
as a potential learning tool

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:20.190
and even in other cases,

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:23.850
a potential learning obstacle, I think,

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:25.500
more thinking about what it means

00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:28.650
to designate it as artificial
is really warranted

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:31.284
and what it means to group a
lot of these tools together

00:28:31.284 --> 00:28:32.640
as some kind of thing

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:34.470
that's different than
the normal way we learn.

00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:38.760
Because, as Professor
Wexler's comments indicated,

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:41.164
all the way back to writing
is an initial practice

00:28:41.164 --> 00:28:44.108
that we might think about as a technology

00:28:44.108 --> 00:28:46.620
which dramatically
changed the way we think,

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:49.380
and communicate, and understand ourselves,

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:53.790
and so breaking apart that
category of artificial

00:28:53.790 --> 00:28:56.010
and thinking through it a
little bit more granularly,

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:58.562
and where some of these
deeper conversations

00:28:58.562 --> 00:29:01.410
that we're having in lots
of areas of our culture

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:02.670
are maybe feeding this,

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:05.400
and less focused, perhaps,
on the particulars.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:06.366
This one area

00:29:06.366 --> 00:29:09.570
that I think would be really
beneficial for students

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:10.923
and for faculty as well,

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:16.680
but the flip side of that is,

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:19.320
in fact, I think we do need a
lot more particular knowledge

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:20.610
about the specific tools,

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:22.410
but not so much...

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:24.390
Not only or not not limited

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:28.590
to the way in which the
algorithms work or don't work,

00:29:28.590 --> 00:29:30.750
and not only limited to the degree

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:32.940
to which we can detect particular forms

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:34.560
of bias circulating in them,

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:36.180
but this is a perspective that I learned

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:39.250
from colleagues who work

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:43.770
in this field of history
of science and technology.

00:29:43.770 --> 00:29:47.790
The kind of work that I
know about from them is

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:52.790
so focused on questions of how
technological tools are made.

00:29:54.750 --> 00:29:55.830
Who pays for them?

00:29:55.830 --> 00:29:58.784
What interests are involved
in the construction of them?

00:29:58.784 --> 00:30:02.091
How our own engagement with them benefits

00:30:02.091 --> 00:30:05.430
the potential projects
that may be in development,

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:07.590
and all of those kinds of questions

00:30:07.590 --> 00:30:08.850
are so essential to knowing

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:11.070
how we would be able to
actually use the tool,

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:14.820
almost like really
detailed social biographies

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:17.160
of particular tools we might be using.

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:19.860
That seems like something that
would be essential to have,

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:23.970
and, I think, the
difficulties in doing that

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:26.730
are both what some people
have already mentioned

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:27.870
about the corporate interests

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:29.940
and how accessible all that information is

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:30.810
to the public eye,

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:32.700
and the fact that these
are often corporations

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:33.533
who are running them.

00:30:33.533 --> 00:30:34.830
That's not been given

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:37.440
that we would have access
to that kind of information.

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:38.273
Excuse me.

00:30:38.273 --> 00:30:43.253
But also, I think something
that's making that difficult is

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:50.760
simply the pace

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:53.460
at which these particular
tools are developing,

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:55.320
and so, what would that mean to do that

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:59.907
for each example,

00:30:59.907 --> 00:31:02.280
and each genre of artificial intelligence

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:04.820
that's going to be
inundating us in the coming,

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:08.430
however many years that
this boom is happening?

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:11.070
And so, what would that mean
to do that in each case?

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:12.330
It really does seem to me

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:15.540
that a required course
in history of technology,

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:17.099
history and sociology of technology,

00:31:17.099 --> 00:31:19.500
would need to be something

00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:21.330
that the university should think about,

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:23.790
so that everyone would feel that...

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:27.060
Before we know how we
even wanna talk about

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:28.440
what creativity means,

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:29.610
or what originality means,

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:31.110
or what cheating would even mean

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:32.490
in relation to a tool like that,

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:33.790
which is not at all clear,

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:37.304
we have a sense of what
it is we're dealing with

00:31:37.304 --> 00:31:42.000
in a really academic,
scholarly rich sense,

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:44.211
and at present, that doesn't
seem to be widely available.

00:31:44.211 --> 00:31:46.293
So that's kind of where I'm coming from.

00:31:47.995 --> 00:31:50.610
- Thank you so much, Professor Khawaja,

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:51.903
and all the panelists,

00:31:53.460 --> 00:31:56.070
we're gonna spend 25 minutes
hashing this out further,

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:58.530
but I feel like there's so
much to think about already.

00:31:58.530 --> 00:32:00.186
Even if we all just went off and wrote,

00:32:00.186 --> 00:32:03.120
we would get a lot further
than we'd been alone.

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:05.880
I'm gonna make one very
sweeping observation,

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:07.770
which everyone in the
audience might not need,

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:09.810
but I just feel some
pressure as the moderator

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:11.550
to say something like this.

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:15.270
So we got a lot of
questions during the chat

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:17.154
and I'm gonna bring up
as many of them as I can,

00:32:17.154 --> 00:32:20.730
but I noticed that some of them refer back

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:23.143
to a distinction or separation

00:32:23.143 --> 00:32:25.110
that Professor Vishnoi
always started us with,

00:32:25.110 --> 00:32:28.203
which is the space between
evaluation and learning.

00:32:29.414 --> 00:32:31.260
In the middle of the panel,

00:32:31.260 --> 00:32:33.210
we talked a lot about

00:32:33.210 --> 00:32:35.280
the opportunities for learning and growth,

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:37.530
and T newness that this
technology opens up,

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:39.295
and how can we seize those

00:32:39.295 --> 00:32:41.881
by approaching it with a certain mindset.

00:32:41.881 --> 00:32:43.680
So I'd say these are two,

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:44.790
and then the third,

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:46.560
which I think Professor Khawaja spoke

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:49.200
to the most directly is

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:51.370
what she called the
ontological uncertainty

00:32:51.370 --> 00:32:53.188
of these things happening

00:32:53.188 --> 00:32:55.440
at this particular historical moment

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:58.770
with our particular social,
cultural, economic structures,

00:32:58.770 --> 00:33:02.430
and so I'm just noticing
those three as big themes

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:04.260
that if this were a classroom,

00:33:04.260 --> 00:33:05.100
we were gonna write a paper,

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:08.130
I'd say, "Pick one of these
three themes and do X,"

00:33:08.130 --> 00:33:09.060
and then you'd submit it.

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:10.953
No, I won't make a ChatGPT joke.

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:12.623
So with that said,

00:33:12.623 --> 00:33:16.598
I'm gonna start with one or
two questions that came in

00:33:16.598 --> 00:33:20.280
that are a little bit
more simple and concrete,

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:22.077
because I think there are ways,

00:33:22.077 --> 00:33:23.970
and we didn't end up talking about them,

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:24.803
but we'll see.

00:33:24.803 --> 00:33:26.940
I mentioned this to the panel in advance.

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:29.040
I'm hoping, even if you
don't feel like an expert,

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:30.930
that one or at most two people

00:33:30.930 --> 00:33:33.240
will take a swing at
some of these questions,

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:35.910
and so the first one actually
came in ahead of the panel

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:37.451
and it may seem like
it doesn't really fit,

00:33:37.451 --> 00:33:38.960
but I think it's very important.

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:41.874
It's essentially about evaluation

00:33:41.874 --> 00:33:43.590
and it's about...

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:45.390
We talked about practices for teaching

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:46.590
and how they might have to change,

00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:49.680
and we talked about understanding
this moment more deeply

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:52.410
so that students could
direct their own change,

00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:54.810
but one piece of take home writing

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:58.410
that the university relies
on in a processing way

00:33:58.410 --> 00:33:59.748
are application essays,

00:33:59.748 --> 00:34:01.530
especially, for graduate school,

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:04.140
and so one of the
questions that came in is,

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:05.547
are there practices or ways

00:34:05.547 --> 00:34:08.700
that we could think profitably about

00:34:08.700 --> 00:34:10.050
using application essays

00:34:10.050 --> 00:34:12.510
for professional schools like
medical school and law school?

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:14.370
And we didn't really talk about that,

00:34:14.370 --> 00:34:15.570
because I think, appropriately,

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:17.190
we were thinking about teaching

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:19.194
from the moment of, students are with us,

00:34:19.194 --> 00:34:23.040
but I wonder if anyone
wants to start with that

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:23.970
and then I'll just say,

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:24.990
who wants to do a follow up?

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:26.993
And then we'll go on to another question.

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:29.910
- [Justin] Alfie, can you
expand on that a little bit?

00:34:29.910 --> 00:34:31.500
- Oh, absolutely, Justin.

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:33.700
Thank you very
- Yeah, just what you mean--

00:34:34.616 --> 00:34:36.130
- I guess...

00:34:36.130 --> 00:34:38.093
How I understand the question is,

00:34:38.093 --> 00:34:39.603
once students are here,

00:34:39.603 --> 00:34:42.450
we can do things with their
engagement with software

00:34:42.450 --> 00:34:44.070
like this.

00:34:44.070 --> 00:34:46.830
You gave some very
powerful, concrete examples

00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:49.650
of ways you can have
students look at the output

00:34:49.650 --> 00:34:53.250
and do critical reading
as a way of learning more.

00:34:53.250 --> 00:34:55.170
We can talk to them about the difference

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:56.910
between what the machine can produce

00:34:56.910 --> 00:34:59.100
and what some students
in the class can produce,

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:01.260
and help them strive for that,

00:35:01.260 --> 00:35:02.910
but when students are
submitting things to us

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:05.751
that they've produced at home
before they're even students

00:35:05.751 --> 00:35:08.820
and it's hard for us to
tell to what degree they did

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:10.743
or did not collaborate with AI,

00:35:11.586 --> 00:35:14.970
are there any practices or
ways that we could think about

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:18.060
either how to feel or read those materials

00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:22.620
or are there different ways we
could ask questions of people

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:24.450
who are applying to come to Yale,

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:27.960
than essentially, "Send
us a take home essay,

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:31.620
answer this question that
we've crafted for you?"

00:35:31.620 --> 00:35:33.150
That's where I think
the question comes from

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:36.360
and I think it's a work process anxiety.

00:35:36.360 --> 00:35:37.650
There's a thing thing we do.

00:35:37.650 --> 00:35:39.000
It takes a lot of time.

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:40.080
It's an important part

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:42.987
of how we get our students here on campus

00:35:42.987 --> 00:35:44.670
and it seems that this technology

00:35:44.670 --> 00:35:45.777
will immediately disrupt it.

00:35:45.777 --> 00:35:48.840
So I don't know if anyone
on the panel feels like

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:52.140
you even just want to give
us a mantra to think about,

00:35:52.140 --> 00:35:53.070
if not practice,

00:35:53.070 --> 00:35:55.770
but it seems like a fair question to me.

00:35:55.770 --> 00:35:57.720
What about the materials

00:35:57.720 --> 00:36:00.000
that come before we even
have the students with us,

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.319
so that we can engage them differently?

00:36:01.319 --> 00:36:03.496
Yeah, Professor Wexler.

00:36:03.496 --> 00:36:07.530
- Well, isn't this a
question of evaluation?

00:36:07.530 --> 00:36:11.149
Isn't this the same
question of evaluation?

00:36:11.149 --> 00:36:16.149
And I hesitate, since it's
really a very time consuming,

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:19.587
professional activity,

00:36:19.587 --> 00:36:23.850
and I'm not doing it to
make a statement about it,

00:36:23.850 --> 00:36:27.471
but the further philosophical question is,

00:36:27.471 --> 00:36:31.470
evaluating for what?

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:35.790
We really have now to know

00:36:35.790 --> 00:36:38.760
what the purpose of these years

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:42.480
that we wanna spend with
the applicants would be.

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:44.610
Evaluating for what?

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:47.760
And I do think this is disruptive.

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:50.103
There are many opportunities
for disruption.

00:36:52.052 --> 00:36:55.868
I would hesitate to say what they are,

00:36:55.868 --> 00:36:59.970
but evaluation is one of the terms

00:36:59.970 --> 00:37:01.680
that comes under pressure.

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:03.275
What for?

00:37:03.275 --> 00:37:07.230
Did people need to learn to
add, and subtract, and multiply,

00:37:07.230 --> 00:37:09.600
or was the slide rule fine?

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:11.983
Did people need to learn

00:37:11.983 --> 00:37:15.210
to write their own application to college

00:37:15.210 --> 00:37:20.210
or was learning to use chat AI fine?

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:25.470
And if it was fine, what do we
do with them when they come?

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:30.450
What is it for that they
would be here with us?

00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:32.073
We need to talk about that.

00:37:33.250 --> 00:37:34.620
- Yeah, that's great,

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:35.850
and Professor Farrell,

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:38.490
I think you're offered to
speak back to this too.

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:40.440
- Yeah, I just wanted to
build on Professor Wexler

00:37:40.440 --> 00:37:41.370
just very quickly,

00:37:41.370 --> 00:37:42.203
in the sense of,

00:37:42.203 --> 00:37:45.480
I think this is a question of evaluation,

00:37:45.480 --> 00:37:47.970
but I think it's part
of a broader emphasis,

00:37:47.970 --> 00:37:51.000
or at least where we're moving is

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:52.290
not relying on one indicator,

00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:54.360
whether it's the essay or GRE scores,

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:58.350
but a holistic evaluation of
what the student is bringing,

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:00.630
what the university can
offer them as a person,

00:38:00.630 --> 00:38:02.220
what our community can provide them

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:04.920
as they learn with other
students in community.

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:07.530
That's really important as well,

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:09.630
and then obviously
letters of recommendation,

00:38:09.630 --> 00:38:13.413
but hopefully those aren't
written by AI as well.

00:38:13.413 --> 00:38:14.246
Then I don't know.

00:38:14.246 --> 00:38:17.130
(Justin laughing)

00:38:17.130 --> 00:38:18.692
- Professor Khawaja, I
see you have your hand up.

00:38:18.692 --> 00:38:22.585
- I do confess that that
was the silent thought

00:38:22.585 --> 00:38:25.350
that crossed my mind when we
were reading all the headlines

00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:28.410
about students using ChatGPT was,

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:30.659
what about the recommendation side?

00:38:30.659 --> 00:38:34.890
But I also wanna build
on the last two comments.

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:37.662
I think that maybe this is...

00:38:37.662 --> 00:38:38.991
I don't know how this point will go over,

00:38:38.991 --> 00:38:42.120
but at the undergraduate level,

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:44.580
obviously, faculty are not at
all involved in admissions.

00:38:44.580 --> 00:38:46.620
It's a complete black box.

00:38:46.620 --> 00:38:47.580
At the PhD level,

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:49.830
which is all that religious
studies operates at,

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:51.351
we don't operate in an MA,

00:38:51.351 --> 00:38:52.830
I think it's very unlikely

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:55.320
that we would have that kind of an issue

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:58.200
with someone submitting the
kind of extended research

00:38:58.200 --> 00:38:59.970
that you would need

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:03.030
to apply to a PhD program
in the humanities,

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:04.440
but at the undergraduate level,

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:07.230
just to take the spirit
of your question on,

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:10.920
I think, thinking back to
something Professor Vishnoi said

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:13.170
about the use of AI,

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:15.570
one of the positive potential
uses of this kind of thing is

00:39:15.570 --> 00:39:18.690
when there is a very distinct norm

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:22.560
that others are being
asked to follow or to meet,

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:25.530
because what we're looking
at is a norm machine,

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:27.540
like, a patterned repetition of norms

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:30.990
that are already flowing through
whatever they're being fed.

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:32.280
That can be a very effective way,

00:39:32.280 --> 00:39:35.164
and as a faculty concerned about access

00:39:35.164 --> 00:39:38.310
and some of these other things
that we talk about today,

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:39.830
and equity in the university,

00:39:39.830 --> 00:39:42.570
it could be a wonderful thing in a way

00:39:42.570 --> 00:39:45.390
if admissions essays were a
little bit more randomized,

00:39:45.390 --> 00:39:47.670
if like applications were a
little bit more randomized

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:49.440
at the admissions level.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:51.019
I don't think that would
necessarily be a bad thing.

00:39:51.019 --> 00:39:53.353
I would much rather be talking about

00:39:53.353 --> 00:39:55.590
what we would use it for in a classroom

00:39:55.590 --> 00:39:57.030
or what kind of a tool it would be

00:39:57.030 --> 00:39:58.380
than how we would prevent people

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:01.152
from using it to get into the university.

00:40:01.152 --> 00:40:03.273
That could be an interesting hack.

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:07.584
- Yeah, Professor Vishnoi.

00:40:07.584 --> 00:40:11.505
- So I just wanted to add
to what Noreen just said.

00:40:11.505 --> 00:40:14.239
I think it immediately means

00:40:14.239 --> 00:40:18.180
that we have to diversify our evaluation.

00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:21.660
I think what also happens
in these kinds of places,

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:24.120
such as where we are right now, is that,

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:27.960
over time, the evaluation
process has become very stylized

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:31.052
and started benefiting
those who have access

00:40:31.052 --> 00:40:35.193
and privilege to all the resources,

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:39.900
and I think this in general is a bad thing

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:41.160
as far as I'm concerned,

00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:46.160
and so perhaps we need to
just diversify evaluations,

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:47.033
and I think what Noreen is saying,

00:40:47.033 --> 00:40:51.934
that we have to get used to
reading more diverse essays,

00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:57.270
as opposed to all essays
having a very similar flavor,

00:40:57.270 --> 00:41:01.890
and if one of these essays comes
from outside, it feels odd,

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:06.890
and so our tendency is to reject
it or devalue it some way.

00:41:07.230 --> 00:41:08.970
So I think it also helps...

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:10.974
I think, at the end of the day,

00:41:10.974 --> 00:41:12.900
we as evaluators

00:41:12.900 --> 00:41:15.600
will also have to correct
our internal biases.

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:18.030
What exactly are we responding
to with theses essays,

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:19.863
is it content or the style?

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:24.813
And I think that's a challenge for us.

