00:46:48.761 --> 00:46:51.030
So, very basic.

00:46:51.030 --> 00:46:53.270
- So I'll offer two examples up.

00:46:53.270 --> 00:46:54.480
One of the things

00:46:54.480 --> 00:46:57.990
that we often try to train
our students to do is

00:46:57.990 --> 00:47:02.220
to differentiate the
hype about a technology

00:47:02.220 --> 00:47:04.800
from the reality of the technology,

00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:08.520
and ChatGPT is a fantastic example there

00:47:08.520 --> 00:47:10.920
where you can look at the headlines

00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:12.720
and what is claimed about it,

00:47:12.720 --> 00:47:15.540
and then actually work with
the real system directly,

00:47:15.540 --> 00:47:17.370
which is not often something

00:47:17.370 --> 00:47:20.490
that in many cases you're able to do,

00:47:20.490 --> 00:47:22.653
and explore the boundaries yourself,

00:47:24.283 --> 00:47:27.210
and especially, as we're
trying to build students

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:29.580
who are really informed

00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:32.610
about the limits of
where technology can go

00:47:32.610 --> 00:47:36.054
and what the likely implications
of that technology are,

00:47:36.054 --> 00:47:41.054
being able to seek out and
find that critique on your own,

00:47:41.580 --> 00:47:45.030
is a really fantastic experience.

00:47:45.030 --> 00:47:48.930
So we often like to pair
the claims about GPT

00:47:48.930 --> 00:47:53.493
with the experience of GPT
as a great learning tool.

00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:56.454
The second thing that we often use it for,

00:47:56.454 --> 00:48:00.090
honestly, is to address questions

00:48:00.090 --> 00:48:05.090
about the role of the technology

00:48:05.220 --> 00:48:09.183
and issues related to the
responsibility of authorship.

00:48:10.680 --> 00:48:12.270
I saw just last week

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:16.320
that science and nature came
out with two guiding rules

00:48:16.320 --> 00:48:19.260
about how to use ChatGPT

00:48:19.260 --> 00:48:24.153
in similar large language
models in scientific papers,

00:48:25.380 --> 00:48:26.670
and it was very simple.

00:48:26.670 --> 00:48:29.157
It was number one if you use them at all,

00:48:29.157 --> 00:48:31.290
that you must acknowledge them,

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:34.230
and that seems to be a very easy thing

00:48:34.230 --> 00:48:36.330
for everyone to understand,

00:48:36.330 --> 00:48:38.970
but their second rule was that ChatGPT

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:42.453
and similar models can never
be listed as an author,

00:48:43.318 --> 00:48:46.140
and the why for that I think is

00:48:46.140 --> 00:48:48.169
a really great lesson for us,

00:48:48.169 --> 00:48:51.390
and the thing that they
very explicitly said

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:52.350
in their announcement

00:48:52.350 --> 00:48:56.170
was that with authorship
comes a responsibility

00:48:57.240 --> 00:48:59.970
and that none of those language models,

00:48:59.970 --> 00:49:03.510
none of those technologies could
accept that responsibility,

00:49:03.510 --> 00:49:06.150
and so thinking about that in that way

00:49:06.150 --> 00:49:08.472
actually lets us get at some issues about,

00:49:08.472 --> 00:49:12.330
what does it mean for these
systems to produce materials?

00:49:12.330 --> 00:49:14.160
Do we allow them to be copywritten?

00:49:14.160 --> 00:49:16.470
Do we allow them to have authorship?

00:49:16.470 --> 00:49:19.860
Should we allow them to...

00:49:19.860 --> 00:49:21.026
Are they due royalties?

00:49:21.026 --> 00:49:24.459
Should we have robots that work pay taxes?

00:49:24.459 --> 00:49:26.280
There's a lot of questions about this

00:49:26.280 --> 00:49:29.650
that are both at a technology
level and a policy level

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:32.010
that are deep and rich,

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:34.060
and this is a great way to approach them.

