00:51:13.080 --> 00:51:14.880
calls close reading for craft,

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:16.950
and it puts in the foreground

00:51:16.950 --> 00:51:19.110
of teaching writing, looking at texts,

00:51:19.110 --> 00:51:20.217
and thinking about how they work,

00:51:20.217 --> 00:51:21.582
and which things are worth emulating

00:51:21.582 --> 00:51:24.480
and where you want to
exceed or change them,

00:51:24.480 --> 00:51:26.670
and I'll just say in my
own tiny, little class

00:51:26.670 --> 00:51:28.650
of only 12 first years this year,

00:51:28.650 --> 00:51:31.098
I gave ChatGPT one of
the homework assignments,

00:51:31.098 --> 00:51:34.140
and we looked at the example together,

00:51:34.140 --> 00:51:35.220
and talked about,

00:51:35.220 --> 00:51:37.440
what is it doing that you thought

00:51:37.440 --> 00:51:39.030
was one of the things you needed to do

00:51:39.030 --> 00:51:40.350
and what is it not doing

00:51:40.350 --> 00:51:42.810
that you even writing
it for the first time,

00:51:42.810 --> 00:51:43.860
this kind of assignment,

00:51:43.860 --> 00:51:46.118
you sensed needed to be done?

00:51:46.118 --> 00:51:48.720
And how could I write this assignment,

00:51:48.720 --> 00:51:51.060
so that it would ask you for more of that

00:51:51.060 --> 00:51:52.140
and less of the first?

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:53.160
And then we had a discussion,

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:54.840
and I'm not saying we achieved it.

00:51:54.840 --> 00:51:56.940
And like I said, that may
turn out to have been naive,

00:51:56.940 --> 00:51:58.697
but it's a really specific example.

00:51:58.697 --> 00:52:01.050
It wasn't even debating with the model.

00:52:01.050 --> 00:52:04.273
It was looking at it as a
piece of language constructed

00:52:04.273 --> 00:52:06.993
and then thinking about, what can it do?

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:09.820
I really just mean that as an example.

00:52:11.940 --> 00:52:14.067
If one occurs to me from this page,

00:52:14.067 --> 00:52:15.870
I'll pop one more question in there,

00:52:15.870 --> 00:52:17.760
but I think what I'd like to say is,

00:52:17.760 --> 00:52:19.080
some of you didn't respond

00:52:19.080 --> 00:52:20.670
to questions during the question period,

00:52:20.670 --> 00:52:21.503
and is there something

00:52:21.503 --> 00:52:23.850
that you were thinking
during the rest of the panel

00:52:23.850 --> 00:52:27.363
or some last word you want to
give us, our last question,

00:52:28.410 --> 00:52:30.390
just to give you one more
shot to say something?

00:52:30.390 --> 00:52:32.140
I'm speaking to the panelists here.

00:52:33.150 --> 00:52:34.770
- I just wanna say, in the materials

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:39.770
that Jenny Frederick put
on the Poorvu website,

00:52:39.780 --> 00:52:42.240
there was one person
who said that her dream

00:52:42.240 --> 00:52:47.240
was that her university
would make a week available

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:51.685
for faculty to go to computer camp.

00:52:51.685 --> 00:52:53.730
(Laura laughing)

00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:57.180
And let us learn together.

00:52:57.180 --> 00:52:59.103
I thought that was a great idea.

00:53:01.418 --> 00:53:04.020
- I'm gonna add a reflection on...

00:53:04.020 --> 00:53:06.720
I'm gonna say it's on what
Professor Khawaja was discussing,

00:53:06.720 --> 00:53:08.640
although there was a buildup.

00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:10.350
Since she synthesized a little bit,

00:53:10.350 --> 00:53:11.370
there was a buildup to it,

00:53:11.370 --> 00:53:16.370
but I'm thinking about
how I have to take some...

00:53:17.100 --> 00:53:18.090
Maybe I don't have to do anything,

00:53:18.090 --> 00:53:18.923
maybe I don't need to overreact,

00:53:18.923 --> 00:53:21.210
but I'm doing some actions

00:53:21.210 --> 00:53:23.430
in relationship with this new technology

00:53:23.430 --> 00:53:26.550
while trying to understand
the historical moment,

00:53:26.550 --> 00:53:31.454
and I don't know if you have
any advice on how to pause

00:53:31.454 --> 00:53:36.454
or what a pattern of act
and reflect might look like,

00:53:36.900 --> 00:53:39.623
but this is a thing I
find myself anxious about.

00:53:39.623 --> 00:53:41.823
So turning to you.

00:53:45.740 --> 00:53:50.250
- There is no pause, not in Yale,

00:53:50.250 --> 00:53:55.250
and not in the world that Yale
is feeding and be fed from,

00:53:55.290 --> 00:54:00.290
but if we were really
serious about doing it,

00:54:00.450 --> 00:54:01.890
we would make one.

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:03.090
We would make that week.

00:54:03.090 --> 00:54:04.080
We would make that pause.

00:54:04.080 --> 00:54:06.240
We would require the class

00:54:06.240 --> 00:54:08.367
that maybe doesn't teach the details

00:54:08.367 --> 00:54:11.820
of how a particular technological entity

00:54:11.820 --> 00:54:13.050
that we're dealing with is made,

00:54:13.050 --> 00:54:15.489
but teaches at a minimum,

00:54:15.489 --> 00:54:17.730
students how to ask the questions

00:54:17.730 --> 00:54:18.563
that they would need to know

00:54:18.563 --> 00:54:21.780
and where to look for
that kind of information.

00:54:21.780 --> 00:54:24.000
Us as well, I think we'd
probably need a computer camp

00:54:24.000 --> 00:54:26.913
and also a history of
tech camp for all of us.

00:54:28.930 --> 00:54:31.003
- Professor Scassellati.

00:54:32.630 --> 00:54:34.920
- So I will add two things to that.

00:54:34.920 --> 00:54:37.210
One quickly is just that

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:42.330
the dean's office actually has a series

00:54:42.330 --> 00:54:45.720
of small, by faculty for faculty classes

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:47.100
at the end of the year,

00:54:47.100 --> 00:54:49.170
and over the last three years,

00:54:49.170 --> 00:54:51.570
we have offered an AI for faculty

00:54:51.570 --> 00:54:54.840
or a natural language for faculty course.

00:54:54.840 --> 00:54:56.190
So we are actually hoping

00:54:56.190 --> 00:54:58.720
to try to address that in many ways

00:54:59.640 --> 00:55:03.870
and we've been also struggling
to try to find a way

00:55:03.870 --> 00:55:07.170
to address this systemically
at the undergraduate level,

00:55:07.170 --> 00:55:09.910
because we see AI tools coming out

00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:14.680
that are impacting every
possible area of inquiry

00:55:15.540 --> 00:55:17.905
in ways that we cannot anticipate,

00:55:17.905 --> 00:55:20.850
and in many cases,

00:55:20.850 --> 00:55:25.850
did not even guess at in
the years ahead of time.

00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:28.440
So starting next year,

00:55:28.440 --> 00:55:32.340
we're hoping to offer an
AI course for non-majors

00:55:32.340 --> 00:55:34.173
with no programming required,

00:55:35.940 --> 00:55:39.420
and look for more details
on that hopefully soon.

00:55:39.420 --> 00:55:42.008
I'm hoping that it gets approved.

00:55:42.008 --> 00:55:43.650
The last thing I would say is

00:55:43.650 --> 00:55:45.760
that we've talked a lot about

00:55:46.860 --> 00:55:50.220
what are the boundary cases and places

00:55:50.220 --> 00:55:53.880
where we don't think that this
technology would be useful,

00:55:53.880 --> 00:55:56.610
but reflecting back on the announcements

00:55:56.610 --> 00:55:58.620
from science and nature,

00:55:58.620 --> 00:55:59.940
they acknowledged the case

00:55:59.940 --> 00:56:02.550
that there were very good, potential uses

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:04.290
for this technology,

00:56:04.290 --> 00:56:05.460
and not only as...

00:56:05.460 --> 00:56:07.170
Nisheeth had mentioned

00:56:07.170 --> 00:56:10.350
that this was a way of leveling the field,

00:56:10.350 --> 00:56:13.650
but that there may be genuinely
good places to use this.

00:56:13.650 --> 00:56:17.610
So I think one of the challenging
questions I'm left with

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:19.260
after this panel is,

00:56:19.260 --> 00:56:20.430
what are the places

00:56:20.430 --> 00:56:23.040
where we would want the
use of this technology

00:56:23.040 --> 00:56:24.780
in our courses?

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:26.550
What are the positive cases

00:56:26.550 --> 00:56:30.360
where that would be seen
as a very useful addition

00:56:30.360 --> 00:56:33.810
and can we define some rules
and some boundary cases

00:56:33.810 --> 00:56:37.773
to say that beyond that is
maybe not what we want to see?

00:56:38.670 --> 00:56:42.303
So maybe we can come up
with some of those as well.

00:56:45.735 --> 00:56:48.360
- I have to say, I love going
out on a potential high note.

00:56:48.360 --> 00:56:51.480
So thank you for making me
feel a little more cheerful.

00:56:51.480 --> 00:56:52.980
Thank you so much to the panel.

00:56:52.980 --> 00:56:55.080
I feel really moved and daunted

00:56:55.080 --> 00:56:57.120
by your wisdom and care about this,

00:56:57.120 --> 00:57:00.300
and thank you for
everyone who came to show

00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:01.710
that you wanna learn more about this,

00:57:01.710 --> 00:57:05.520
and do best by our
students and our culture.

00:57:05.520 --> 00:57:07.200
So that's it for today.

00:57:07.200 --> 00:57:09.330
We will be following up with other events

00:57:09.330 --> 00:57:11.580
and we're very grateful to everyone.

00:57:11.580 --> 00:57:12.413
Take care.

00:57:12.413 --> 00:57:14.797
(upbeat music)

